{"schemaVersion":1,"source":{"title":"2026 Adopted Budget, Town of Riverhead, line by line from the 2027 Budget Supplement","detail":"Every account line of all 19 funds, as the Town reprints its adopted budget in the next year’s Budget Supplement (read by etl/parse_supplement_history.py). Each year shown adds up to its budget’s Summary page, fund by fund, on spending and on revenue.","url":"https://www.townofriverheadny.gov/DocumentCenter/View/3929/2027-Budget-Supplement-PDF"},"payrollYear":2025,"census":{"dataset":"American Community Survey 2023 5-year estimates (2019–2023)","source":{"title":"U.S. Census Bureau, American Community Survey 2023 5-Year Estimates, Data Profiles","url":"https://data.census.gov/table?g=060XX00US3610361984","detail":"Retrieved from the Census Data API. Five-year estimates are used because Riverhead is far below the 65,000-population threshold for one-year estimates."},"population":36394,"populationSource":"U.S. Census Bureau, Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Minor Civil Divisions in New York (SUB-EST2025), July 1, 2025","households":13678,"householdsMoe":571,"medianHouseholdIncome":93595},"programs":[{"key":"3","name":"Public Safety","plain":"Police, code enforcement, fire protection, the building inspectors and the dog control officer.","narrative":"Riverhead runs its own police department, and that single choice dominates the Town budget. Once pension and health insurance for sworn officers are counted, Public Safety is the largest thing the Town does by a wide margin: 36% of everything in the 2026 adopted budget. The rest of the function is smaller but visible: the building inspectors who sign off on your permit, code enforcement, the Town’s contribution to fire protection, the Bay Constable, the Juvenile Aid Bureau and the Anti-Bias Task Force.","buys":["A full-service police department: patrol squads, detectives, dispatch, traffic and K-9","Building inspection and code enforcement","Fire protection and the Fire Marshal","Bay Constable and harbormaster","Juvenile Aid Bureau, Youth Court and the Anti-Bias Task Force","Animal control"],"direct":24798240,"benefits":18740318.789719254,"fullCost":43538558.789719254,"earned":2691200,"net":40847358.789719254,"recoveryPct":6.18118760659453,"netPerResident":1122.3651917821414,"netPerHousehold":2986.3546417399657,"staff":171,"departments":[{"fund":"A01","code":"3120","name":"Police","amount":22388906},{"fund":"A01","code":"3625","name":"Code Enforcement","amount":671254},{"fund":"A01","code":"3620","name":"Building (Safety Inspection)","amount":521209},{"fund":"A01","code":"3410","name":"Fire Protection","amount":520986},{"fund":"A01","code":"3510","name":"Control of Dogs","amount":332900},{"fund":"A01","code":"3125","name":"Juv Aid -Juvenile Aid Bureau","amount":160841},{"fund":"A01","code":"3122","name":"Bay Con -Bay Constable","amount":153209},{"fund":"A04","code":"3120","name":"Police","amount":34560},{"fund":"A01","code":"3126","name":"Youth Court Department","amount":9675},{"fund":"A01","code":"3989","name":"Anti-Bias Task Force","amount":2500},{"fund":"A04","code":"3125","name":"Juv Aid -Juvenile Aid Bureau","amount":2200}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Building Inspection Fees","amount":1200000},{"name":"Rental Application Fees","amount":375000},{"name":"Fire Inspection Fees","amount":300000},{"name":"Police Fees - SRO Reimbursement","amount":160000},{"name":"Suffolk County E911 Aid","amount":125000},{"name":"Electrical Inspection Fees","amount":110000}]},{"key":"8","name":"Home & Community Services","plain":"Water, sewer, scavenger waste, refuse collection, planning and zoning.","narrative":"This is where the Town behaves least like a government and most like a utility. Water, the two sewer districts and the scavenger waste plant are billed to the properties they serve, so the function earns back 63% of its cost in the 2026 adopted budget. Refuse collection is paid for by its own district tax, which this page counts with the other taxes rather than as a fee. The districts pay their own insurance and depreciation, which is why those show up here rather than under general government. The tax-supported remainder is the planning and zoning side: the Planning Department, the Zoning Board of Appeals, community development, environmental control and the seed clam program.","buys":["Public water supply and distribution","Two sewer districts and the scavenger waste treatment plant","Refuse and garbage collection","Planning Department and Zoning Board of Appeals","Community Development Agency","Environmental control and the seed clam program"],"direct":22705992,"benefits":2789622.7754941215,"fullCost":25495614.77549412,"earned":16069887,"net":9425727.775494121,"recoveryPct":63.03000394972259,"netPerResident":258.9912561272221,"netPerHousehold":689.1159362110046,"staff":82,"departments":[{"fund":"SR1","code":"8160","name":"Refuse & Garbage","amount":5097076},{"fund":"EW1","code":"8320","name":"Source of Water Supply & Power","amount":3491700},{"fund":"ES1","code":"8130","name":"Sewer Treatment & Disposal","amount":2401500},{"fund":"ES1","code":"1994","name":"Depreciation","amount":2150000},{"fund":"EW1","code":"1994","name":"Depreciation","amount":1850000},{"fund":"EW1","code":"8310","name":"Water AdmWater Administration","amount":1820140},{"fund":"ES5","code":"8189","name":"Scavenger Waste","amount":1590431},{"fund":"ES1","code":"8110","name":"Sewer Administration","amount":850015},{"fund":"A01","code":"8686","name":"CDA -Community Development Administratio","amount":771565},{"fund":"ES3","code":"1994","name":"Depreciation","amount":645000},{"fund":"A01","code":"8020","name":"Planning Department","amount":561495},{"fund":"A01","code":"8160","name":"Refuse -Refuse & Garbage","amount":481427},{"fund":"ES3","code":"8130","name":"Sewer Treatment & Disposal","amount":405943},{"fund":"EW1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":164400},{"fund":"ES1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":119400},{"fund":"ES5","code":"1994","name":"Depreciation","amount":100000},{"fund":"SR1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":79700},{"fund":"A01","code":"8010","name":"Zoning Board of Appeals","amount":46600},{"fund":"ES5","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":31900},{"fund":"ES3","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":21500},{"fund":"A01","code":"8025","name":"Seed Clam -Seed Clam Program","amount":15000},{"fund":"A01","code":"8090","name":"Environmental Control","amount":11200}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Metered Water Sales","amount":5804577},{"name":"Sewer Rents","amount":4838525},{"name":"Sewer Charges","amount":2121888},{"name":"Water Surcharge","amount":900000},{"name":"Sewer Rents","amount":839757},{"name":"Rental Income - Wireless","amount":800000}]},{"key":"1","name":"General Government Support","plain":"Running the Town itself — the offices, the records, the lawyers, the buildings.","narrative":"This is the machinery every other service runs on top of. It pays the Town Board and Supervisor, the Town Clerk who holds the records, the Assessors who value every property in Town, the Tax Receiver who collects on them, the Attorney who defends the Town, the Justice Court, and the finance and purchasing staff who move the money. It also keeps Town Hall standing, heats it, insures it, and runs the IT that everything else depends on. Almost none of it can be charged to a user, so in the 2026 adopted budget it recovers about 5 cents on the dollar.","buys":["Town Board, Supervisor, Town Clerk and the public record","Assessment and tax collection","Town Attorney, Justice Court and court officers","Finance, audit, purchasing and the personnel office","Town Hall, the municipal garage, fuel and information technology","Town-wide insurance, workers’ compensation and risk retention"],"direct":15775750,"benefits":2408384.4006368765,"fullCost":18184134.400636878,"earned":953250,"net":17230884.400636878,"recoveryPct":5.242207184558719,"netPerResident":473.45398693842054,"netPerHousehold":1259.7517473780433,"staff":112,"departments":[{"fund":"A01","code":"1625","name":"Building & Grounds","amount":2239916},{"fund":"A01","code":"1680","name":"Information Technology","amount":1567415},{"fund":"A01","code":"1620","name":"Town Hall Operations","amount":1538765},{"fund":"A01","code":"1420","name":"Town Attorney","amount":1429097},{"fund":"A01","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":1092200},{"fund":"MS1","code":"1710","name":"Workers Comp","amount":1050000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1310","name":"Finance","amount":977942},{"fund":"A01","code":"1110","name":"Just Crt -Town Justice Court","amount":907513},{"fund":"A01","code":"1670","name":"Muni Fuel -Municipal Fuel","amount":740000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1440","name":"Town Engineer","amount":638674},{"fund":"A01","code":"1355","name":"Assmnt -Assessment","amount":563314},{"fund":"MS2","code":"1930","name":"Risk Retention","amount":450000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1690","name":"Municipal Garage","amount":407877},{"fund":"A01","code":"1220","name":"Town Supervisor","amount":407084},{"fund":"A01","code":"1410","name":"Town Clerk","amount":307219},{"fund":"A01","code":"1010","name":"Town Board","amount":295301},{"fund":"A01","code":"1330","name":"Tax Collection","amount":261537},{"fund":"A01","code":"1345","name":"Central Purchasing","amount":221368},{"fund":"A01","code":"1430","name":"Personnel Officer","amount":217834},{"fund":"A01","code":"1320","name":"Auditor","amount":215000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1989","name":"Other Genrl Gov't","amount":134644},{"fund":"A01","code":"1950","name":"Taxes & Asses. On Prop","amount":85000},{"fund":"CM4","code":"1320","name":"Auditor","amount":20000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1480","name":"Public Information & Services","amount":3100},{"fund":"A01","code":"1626","name":"Grumman Memorial","amount":3000},{"fund":"A01","code":"1920","name":"Municipal Assoc. Dues","amount":1950}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Fines and Forfeited Bail","amount":500000},{"name":"Rental Income - 6 West Second St","amount":245900},{"name":"Rental Income - Wireless","amount":64900},{"name":"Rental Income - Veterans Mem Park","amount":60000},{"name":"Yard Waste Permits","amount":35000},{"name":"Civil Penalties","amount":10000}]},{"key":"5","name":"Transportation","plain":"Roads, snow, streetlights and public parking.","narrative":"The Highway Fund keeps roughly 200 centerline miles of Town road passable — resurfacing, patching, drainage, brush, and the plows and sanders that come out overnight in a storm. The Street Lighting District pays the power bill and maintains the fixtures; the Public Parking District covers the downtown lots. Almost none of this is charged to users, so it is carried by the Highway Fund’s own tax levy. What the Town spends per mile is worth comparing against the other nine Suffolk towns.","buys":["General road repairs, resurfacing and drainage","Snow removal and storm response","Highway machinery and the fleet behind it","Street lighting town-wide","Downtown public parking"],"direct":6367825,"benefits":2868842.470798162,"fullCost":9236667.47079816,"earned":30000,"net":9206667.47079816,"recoveryPct":0.3247924654086051,"netPerResident":252.97212372364018,"netPerHousehold":673.1004145926422,"staff":52,"departments":[{"fund":"DA1","code":"5110","name":"General Repairs","amount":3553226},{"fund":"SL1","code":"5182","name":"Street -Street Lighting","amount":748298},{"fund":"DA1","code":"5130","name":"Mach -Machinery","amount":728090},{"fund":"A01","code":"5010","name":"Highway Administration","amount":389711},{"fund":"DA1","code":"5140","name":"Miscellaneous","amount":384000},{"fund":"DA1","code":"5142","name":"Snow Removal Highway","amount":300000},{"fund":"ST1","code":"5650","name":"Public Parking District","amount":126600},{"fund":"DA1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":119000},{"fund":"SL1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":14700},{"fund":"ST1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":3200},{"fund":"ST1","code":"1950","name":"Taxes & Asses. On Prope","amount":1000}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Street Opening Permits","amount":25000},{"name":"Other Transportation Dept Income","amount":5000}]},{"key":"7","name":"Culture & Recreation","plain":"Recreation, parks, beaches, youth and senior programs, the marina and the Town Historian.","narrative":"The most visible thing the Town does per dollar spent, and one of the few that pays much of its own way — recreation programs, beach passes, facility rentals, marina slips and parking permits together return 54% of what the function costs in the 2026 adopted budget. Its staff count is large next to its dollars: 134 people were paid in 2025, the second most of any program, because summer recreation and beach staffing run on a large seasonal and part-time workforce.","buys":["Recreation programs, instruction and adult leagues","Parks, playgrounds and recreation centres","Town beaches","Youth programs, the Teen Center and youth sports","Senior programs and home aid","Marinas and docks","Town Historian and historical properties"],"direct":1805301,"benefits":363441.6893982011,"fullCost":2168742.689398201,"earned":1164550,"net":1004192.6893982012,"recoveryPct":53.6970109775055,"netPerResident":27.5922594218333,"netPerHousehold":73.41663177351961,"staff":134,"departments":[{"fund":"A01","code":"7020","name":"Recreation Administration","amount":630273},{"fund":"A06","code":"7310","name":"Youth Programs","amount":228200},{"fund":"A01","code":"7180","name":"Beaches","amount":199200},{"fund":"A01","code":"7110","name":"Parks","amount":177000},{"fund":"CM2","code":"7230","name":"Marinas & Docs","amount":102613},{"fund":"A01","code":"7610","name":"Senior Programs","amount":89335},{"fund":"A06","code":"7624","name":"Rec InstRec Instructional Programs","amount":65000},{"fund":"A01","code":"7520","name":"Historical Properties","amount":51600},{"fund":"A01","code":"7310","name":"Youth Programs","amount":33500},{"fund":"A01","code":"7611","name":"Home Aid Senior Programs","amount":30000},{"fund":"CM2","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":29800},{"fund":"A04","code":"7314","name":"Lacrosse Program","amount":26350},{"fund":"A06","code":"7315","name":"Other Youth Programs","amount":25000},{"fund":"A06","code":"7621","name":"Recreation","amount":25000},{"fund":"A06","code":"7110","name":"Parks","amount":20000},{"fund":"A01","code":"7140","name":"Playgrounds & Recreation Cente","amount":15900},{"fund":"A06","code":"7620","name":"Adult Recreation","amount":13000},{"fund":"A04","code":"7312","name":"In-Town Soccer Program","amount":11950},{"fund":"A01","code":"7510","name":"Town Historian","amount":11150},{"fund":"A06","code":"7020","name":"Recreation Administration","amount":10000},{"fund":"A04","code":"7625","name":"Softball Programs","amount":5000},{"fund":"A01","code":"7989","name":"Teen Center","amount":2000},{"fund":"A06","code":"7626","name":"Rec Non-League Sports Pro","amount":2000},{"fund":"A04","code":"7313","name":"Travel Soccer Program","amount":1430}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Other Youth Program Fees","amount":320000},{"name":"Rental Income","amount":280000},{"name":"Beach Stickers","amount":230000},{"name":"Field Rental Fee","amount":75000},{"name":"Instructional Program Fees","amount":55000},{"name":"Child Care Rent","amount":52000}]},{"key":"4","name":"Health","plain":"The ambulance district, the registrar of births and deaths, and narcotics guidance.","narrative":"Small in dollars, and almost entirely the Ambulance District — a separate taxing district that pays for emergency medical response. The rest is the Registrar of Vital Statistics, who issues the birth and death certificates residents actually come to Town Hall for, and a contribution to the Narcotics Guidance Council. In the 2026 adopted budget, about 39% of the cost comes back through ambulance billing and certificate fees.","buys":["The Riverhead Ambulance District","Birth, death and marriage records","Narcotics Guidance Council"],"direct":1762824,"benefits":1957.5921813026885,"fullCost":1764781.5921813026,"earned":692200,"net":1072581.5921813026,"recoveryPct":39.222983912951406,"netPerResident":29.47138517836189,"netPerHousehold":78.41655155587824,"staff":0,"departments":[{"fund":"SM1","code":"4540","name":"Ambulance","amount":1679224},{"fund":"SM1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":69600},{"fund":"A01","code":"4210","name":"Narcotics Guidance Council","amount":7500},{"fund":"A01","code":"4020","name":"Registrar of Vital Statisti","amount":6500}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"Ambulance Charges - Medical Billing","amount":640000},{"name":"Death Certificate","amount":42000},{"name":"Birth Certificate","amount":10000},{"name":"Genealogy Fees Town Clerk","amount":200}]},{"key":"6","name":"Economic Assistance & Opportunity","plain":"Senior nutrition and home aid, plus economic development and veterans’ services.","narrative":"Mostly Programs for the Aging — the senior nutrition centre and Meals on Wheels, plus the in-home services that keep older residents out of institutional care. This is the program most dependent on money from somewhere else: state and county aid, SNAP reimbursement and donations cover 24% of its cost in the 2026 adopted budget, so the Town’s own net cost is modest against what it delivers. The remainder is publicity and economic development, and a small line for veterans’ services.","buys":["Senior nutrition centre and Meals on Wheels","EISEP in-home services and residential repair for older residents","Economic development and publicity, including the Business Improvement District","Veterans’ services"],"direct":1245890,"benefits":236463.2817720817,"fullCost":1482353.2817720817,"earned":358000,"net":1124353.2817720817,"recoveryPct":24.150788101742407,"netPerResident":30.89391882651211,"netPerHousehold":82.20158515660782,"staff":23,"departments":[{"fund":"A01","code":"6772","name":"Programs for the Aging","amount":1049154},{"fund":"CM1","code":"6410","name":"Publicity/Economic Developmen","amount":141236},{"fund":"Z14","code":"6980","name":"Economic Development","amount":22700},{"fund":"Z14","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":21400},{"fund":"A01","code":"6410","name":"Publicity/Economic Development","amount":8000},{"fund":"CM1","code":"1910","name":"Unallocated Insurance","amount":2900},{"fund":"A01","code":"6510","name":"Veteran's Services","amount":500}],"topRevenues":[{"name":"EISEP State Aid","amount":175000},{"name":"SNAP Program","amount":100000},{"name":"SNAP Donations (Meals on Wheels)","amount":30000},{"name":"Residential Rehab (IIIB) 90% Fed 10% Cty","amount":20000},{"name":"Nutrition Donation","amount":15000},{"name":"SNAP (Nutrition) Program","amount":10000}]}],"totals":{"direct":74461822,"benefits":27409030.999999996,"fullCost":101870853,"earned":21959087,"net":79911766,"staff":574,"debtService":9890150,"setAside":826751,"interfundTransfers":8523150,"townwideRevenue":99151817,"grandTotal":112587754,"appropriations":121110904,"contingency":826751},"perResident":{"programs":2195.7401219981316,"debtService":271.7522119030609,"everything":2490.209017969995},"perHousehold":{"programs":5842.357508407662,"debtService":723.0698932592484,"everything":6625.87125310718,"shareOfMedianIncome":7.079300446719568},"reconciliation":{"computed":121110904,"appropriations":121110904,"variance":0},"method":[{"title":"The programs are the State’s classification, not ours","body":"New York’s Uniform System of Accounts assigns every municipal dollar to a function, and Riverhead codes to it. Department 3120 is Police because the State says 3120 is Police. The seven headings below are that system, not a grouping this site invented."},{"title":"Revenue is matched to programs by the Town’s own account tag","body":"The last segment of every revenue account carries the same function group as the spending side. Building Inspection Fees end in 3, so they land against Public Safety; Site Plan Fees end in 8 and land against Home & Community Services. Revenue with a letter instead — property tax, sales tax, PILOTs, mortgage tax — belongs to no single program and is reported separately."},{"title":"Pension and health insurance are pushed back onto the programs that earned them","body":"$27.4M of benefits sits in function 9 in the 2026 adopted budget, attached to no service. Accounts ending -UNI- are sworn police and go entirely to Public Safety; -NON- accounts are spread across programs in the same fund by their share of Personal Services. The uniformed/non-uniformed split is the Town’s own coding."},{"title":"Utility overhead follows the utility","body":"Insurance and depreciation booked to function 1 inside a single-purpose fund are the cost of running that utility, not general government, so they follow the fund’s service. This moves $5.4M in the 2026 adopted budget, mostly sewer and water depreciation. The General Fund and the two internal-service funds are left alone."},{"title":"Interfund transfers are excluded","body":"$8.5M of function 9901 in the 2026 adopted budget is one Town fund paying another. Counting it as program spending would double-count real dollars, so it sits outside the program totals."},{"title":"Money set aside is reported on its own","body":"Account 9990, “Fund Balance Contribution”, is money a fund budgets into its savings instead of spending it: $826,751 in the 2026 adopted budget. It pays for no service that year, so it sits outside the program totals. This page used to call it contingency."},{"title":"Every year comes from the same kind of record","body":"The Town prints each adopted budget again, line by line, in the next year’s Budget Supplement, and its Tentative Budget in that year’s own. A year is shown only when those lines add up to the budget’s Summary page for every fund, on both the spending and the revenue side: 2023, 2024, 2025, 2026, 2027 proposed. 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022 are left out because their lines do not add up. A Tentative Budget is the Supervisor’s proposal, not a budget the Board has adopted."},{"title":"Staff counts are the one thing we classified ourselves","body":"Payroll department names are operational units — \"Squad 4 - Police\" — rather than account codes, so mapping them to functions is our work, not the Town’s. Each budget year uses that year’s payroll, or the latest one (2025) for years the payroll has not reached; all 574 people in the 2025 payroll are accounted for. Headcount is bodies on the payroll, not full-time equivalents, so seasonal recreation staff count the same as a full-time clerk."},{"title":"Per-resident and per-household are scale, not a bill","body":"Population is the Census Bureau’s 36,394 estimate; households are 13,678 ± 571 from American Community Survey 2023 5-year estimates (2019–2023). Dividing the Town’s net cost by either one says how big Town government is relative to the people in it, and the same counts divide every year. It is not what you owe — commercial property carries a large share of the levy, and your actual bill depends on your assessment."}],"notCovered":{"title":"What this page still cannot tell you","body":"GFOA’s criteria for a program budget also ask “What are the goals?” For a town, that would mean targets such as response times, permits issued, tons collected or program participation. The Town publishes no performance measures, so there is nothing to report. Cost and revenue are answered here; service-level goals are not, and no amount of rearranging the budget will produce them."},"years":[{"year":2023,"stage":"adopted","label":"2023","sources":{"supplement":{"title":"2024 Budget Supplement","url":"https://www.townofriverheadny.gov/DocumentCenter/View/247/2024-Budget-Supplement-PDF"},"summary":{"title":"2023 Adopted Budget","url":"https://www.townofriverheadny.gov/DocumentCenter/View/2865/2023-Adopted-Budget-PDF"}},"staffYear":2023,"totals":{"direct":6